STEP 1 — On your Ubuntu machine: Download the Firefox Offline Installer
You need the Windows 10, 64‑bit, Firefox ESR Offline Installer (.exe) because ESR is the best choice for stable offline environments and works perfectly with your HTML/JS architecture.

On Ubuntu, run:

wget https://download.mozilla.org/?product=firefox-esr-latest&os=win64&lang=en-US -O firefox_esr_win64.exe
This command:

Downloads the English 64‑bit ESR offline installer for Windows
Saves it as firefox_esr_win64.exe
Requires NO browser on Ubuntu
Requires ONLY basic internet
✔️ STEP 2 — Transfer the Installer to the Windows 10 machine
You have several options depending on your environment:

Option A — USB Flash Drive
Copy from Ubuntu:

cp firefox_esr_win64.exe /media/$USER/YOUR_USB/
Then plug into Windows and copy the installer.

Option B — SCP over LAN
If your Windows box runs OpenSSH server (optional), from Ubuntu:

scp firefox_esr_win64.exe username@windows-ip:/Users/username/Downloads/
Option C — Shared Folder
Mount the shared folder on Ubuntu and copy the file.

Use whichever method your environment permits.

✔️ STEP 3 — Install Firefox ESR on Windows 10
On the Windows machine:

Double‑click firefox_esr_win64.exe
Choose:
Standard Installation or Custom
Ignore any internet‑related sync options
Finish installation
Firefox ESR does not require internet after installation.

✔️ STEP 4 — Disable Auto Updates (important for offline environments)
Open Firefox ESR on Windows:

Click Menu (≡)
Select Settings
Scroll to Firefox Updates
Select: “Check for updates but let you choose to install them” OR “Never check for updates” (offline environments)
This ensures Firefox stays stable and does not request internet.

✔️ STEP 5 — Set Firefox as Default Browser (optional)
Go to:

Windows Settings → Apps → Default Apps → Web Browser → Firefox

This makes sure your dashboard opens in Firefox.
